---
source: crates/trousseau-cli/tests/help.rs
expression: stdout
---
Edit the whole store as a TOML document in your editor.

Opens a temporary plaintext copy in `$VISUAL`, then `$EDITOR`, then `vi`. Save and quit to apply.
Leave the file empty, or quit without changes, to abort. The temporary file is deleted afterward in
every case.

Usage: trousseau edit [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --store <PATH>
          Use this store file. Wins over `--global` and over store discovery
          
          [env: TROUSSEAU_STORE=]

      --global
          Use your personal store instead of the nearest project store (the `.trousseau` file in
          this directory or a parent)

      --identity <PATH>
          An identity (private key) file to try when unlocking. Repeatable. Also tried:
          `TROUSSEAU_IDENTITY_FILE`, the config file's `identity.files`, the default identity file,
          `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` and `~/.ssh/id_rsa`

      --passphrase-file <PATH>
          Read the store's passphrase from this file instead of prompting. One trailing newline is
          stripped

      --json
          Print one JSON document on stdout instead of text. Errors become a JSON object on stderr

      --quiet
          Suppress informational messages on stderr. Errors still print

      --no-input
          Never prompt. A command that would prompt fails instead: exit 4 for a secret, exit 2 for a
          confirmation

The document has one table per entry, keyed by the entry's key:

  ["database/password"]
  value = "s3cr3t"
  env = "PGPASSWORD"                  # optional
  description = "Postgres app role"   # optional

  Add a table to create an entry. Delete a table to remove the entry.
  Binary entries use 'value_base64' instead of 'value'.

Examples:
  Edit with your default editor:
    trousseau edit

  Use VS Code for this one edit (--wait is required):
    EDITOR="code --wait" trousseau edit

Notes:
  edit is the way to change an entry's env name or description without
  retyping its value.
  Some editors keep swap or backup copies of what you open. For vim, add
  to ~/.vimrc:
    autocmd BufNewFile,BufRead trousseau-*.toml
      \ setlocal nobackup nowritebackup noswapfile
